<BaseLayout title="About GrooveNet — Public Vinyl Radio" description="Why Public Vinyl Radio is building GrooveNet: software that augments a physical record collection without replacing it." currentPath="/about">
  <section class="page-hero wrap"><p class="eyebrow">ABOUT / PVR–GN</p><h1>Software in service of the record shelf.</h1><p>GrooveNet comes from the simple observation that a physical collection holds more knowledge than its spine labels can show at a glance.</p></section>
  <section class="manifesto wrap"><article><p class="label">WHY IT EXISTS</p><h2>A collection is more than an inventory.</h2><p>Records carry a personal history: the stores, people, scenes, labels, and nights that brought them together. But a collection also becomes hard to navigate as it grows. GrooveNet makes its musical information searchable and useful without pretending that a database is the collection itself.</p></article><article><p class="label">PHYSICAL, STILL</p><h2>The software is not the listening experience.</h2><p>Audio analysis, metadata, and recommendations can make preparation and discovery sharper. They cannot replace choosing a record, cueing it, or responding to a room. GrooveNet is designed as a technical companion to those practices, not an abstraction away from them.</p></article><article><p class="label">PUBLIC VINYL RADIO</p><h2>A project within a larger practice.</h2><p>Public Vinyl Radio maintains GrooveNet as an open-source project for people who collect, listen, organize, and DJ. The project has its own focus and codebase; its origin is the broader Public Vinyl Radio ecosystem.</p><a class="button" href="https://publicvinylradio.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Visit Public Vinyl Radio ↗</a></article></section>
</BaseLayout>
